---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
const sections = [
  { id: 'website', label: 'Using this website' },
  { id: 'review', label: 'The free public audit' },
  { id: 'agreement', label: 'Paid work and proposals' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'Scope and costs' },
  { id: 'ownership', label: 'Ownership and access' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Expectations and results' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Questions and rights' },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Terms of service"
  description="How enquiries and public audits work, and the matters agreed in writing before any paid Presence Web Services project begins."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="website">
    <h2>1. Using this website</h2>
    <p>
      This website introduces the services offered by Presence Web Services and lets interested
      business owners make an enquiry. It does not provide an online checkout. Submitting the form
      does not purchase a service, start a paid contract or authorise work on your accounts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Please use the website lawfully and provide accurate information. Do not submit passwords,
      harmful content, confidential client records or information you are not entitled to share.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="review">
    <h2>2. The free public audit</h2>
    <p>
      The free audit is a thorough assessment of publicly available business information, such as a
      website or Google Business Profile. It considers usability, service information, visible
      business details and enquiry routes, with findings and recommended priorities. It does not
      cover private systems, invasive security testing, or a complete technical, legal or
      accessibility audit.
    </p>
    <p>
      The audit is free and carries no obligation to purchase. It does not guarantee a response
      within a particular time, availability to undertake a project, improved search rankings or a
      particular business result. Any account access or implementation work is discussed separately.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="agreement">
    <h2>3. Written agreements for paid work</h2>
    <p>
      Before paid work begins, an individual written proposal should confirm the parties, agreed
      services, deliverables, price, tax treatment, timing, access requirements and
      responsibilities. Payment terms, duration, cancellation, notice, complaint handling and any
      relevant liability provisions must be agreed there.
    </p>
    <p>
      These website terms describe the enquiry process. They are not a substitute for the final
      service agreement. No standard minimum contract term, setup fee or cancellation policy is
      imposed by this website.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="scope">
    <h2>4. Scope, quotations and additional costs</h2>
    <p>
      Website prices are starting prices. The final quote depends on the website platform,
      condition, access, complexity and agreed needs. Exact deliverables are agreed before work
      begins.
    </p>
    <p>
      One-off improvements cover a defined set of tasks. An ongoing partnership covers agreed
      continuing support and priorities, rather than unlimited work. New websites, major redesigns,
      extensive redevelopment, complicated repairs and substantial additional projects require a
      separate quotation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hosting, domains, third-party subscriptions and advertising expenditure are separate unless
      explicitly included in the written proposal. Any additional work or charge should be approved
      before it is incurred.
    </p>
    <p>
      Presence does not currently offer large custom software projects, specialist paid advertising
      or enterprise SEO campaigns. Platform limitations or third-party decisions may affect what can
      be delivered.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="ownership">
    <h2>5. Ownership, access and cooperation</h2>
    <p>
      Clients retain ownership of their existing websites, domains and Google Business Profiles.
      Access should be granted using appropriate permissions where supported, with the client
      retaining owner access. Passwords should not be sent through the public enquiry form.
    </p>
    <p>
      The client is responsible for authorising changes, supplying accurate business information and
      ensuring it has permission to use supplied content and images. The proposal should set out how
      access, backups, content approval, any newly created work and handover will be handled.
    </p>
    <p>
      A Google Business Profile is free to own. Presence charges for professional management and
      improvement work. Presence is an independent service. Google controls its own services,
      policies and verification.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="results">
    <h2>6. Realistic expectations</h2>
    <p>
      The purpose of the service is to improve the clarity, accuracy and usefulness of a business’s
      online presence and customer contact journeys. Google rankings, website traffic, enquiries,
      revenue and jobs cannot be guaranteed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Third-party platforms control their own availability, verification, listings and policies.
      Review support concerns authentic customer feedback and professional responses. Presence does
      not offer purchased reviews, rating manipulation or removal of legitimate negative feedback.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reports use available information. A change in a metric does not by itself prove that Presence
      caused a business result.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="questions">
    <h2>7. Questions and legal rights</h2>
    <p>
      If you have a question about these terms, an enquiry or a proposed service, use the{' '}
      <a class="inline-link" href="/contact/">
        contact form
      </a>
      . Personal information is handled as described in the{' '}
      <a class="inline-link" href="/privacy/">
        privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing on this website excludes rights or liabilities that cannot lawfully be excluded. The
      final agreement for paid services should be reviewed for the relevant business circumstances
      and jurisdiction before acceptance.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
